<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Falange Española de las JONS</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FE de las JONS, Falange Española de las Juntas de Ofensiva Nacional Sindicalista, Falange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Falange Española de las JONS was a national syndicalist political party founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1934 by the merger of the Falange Española with the Juntas de Ofensiva Nacional-Sindicalista of Onésimo Redondo and Ramiro Ledesma. Led from October 1934 by José Antonio Primo de Rivera as Jefe Nacional, it set out its programme in the Twenty-Seven Points, and in street violence with the Socialists it formed its own death squads. It won only 0.11% of the votes in the 1936 election. Primo de Rivera was executed in a Republican prison on 20 November 1936, and on 19 April 1937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> merged the party with the Carlists into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fet-y-de-las-jons">FET y de las JONS</a>, the sole legal party of his regime.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party grew out of a small movement under attack. During and after the 1933 election campaign members of both the Falange and the JONS were killed: on 9 February 1934 Matías Montero was murdered while selling Falangist newspapers, and by June 1934 ten were dead, killed mainly by Socialists, who feared that a fascist movement would crush them as had happened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The merged party was first led by a triumvirate of Ramiro Ledesma, Julio Ruiz de Alda and José Antonio Primo de Rivera, with Raimundo Fernández-Cuesta as General Secretary, and it drew intellectuals such as Rafael Sánchez Mazas, Ernesto Giménez Caballero and Dionisio Ridruejo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The historian Martin Blinkhorn distinguishes four strands in the early party: the monarchist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> of Francisco Moreno Herrera, marquis of Eliseda, the authoritarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> of Onésimo Redondo, the radical national syndicalism of Ledesma and the elitist regenerationism of Primo de Rivera. Eliseda, a financial backer, left in November 1934 over the party's view of church and state, and Ledesma was expelled in January 1935. From 1935 the Italian Fascist regime paid the party 50,000 lire a month. Probably never more than ten thousand members strong, the Falange was shunned by other right-wing parties in the 1936 election and ran candidates in 19 of 60 districts.</p><p>Under the Popular Front government sixty-seven Falangists were killed, and the Falange killed sixty-four leftists, mostly socialists and communists; on 16 April 1936 Falangists fired submachine guns at workers in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, killing three. Persecuted by the government, the party adopted cells of three members and joined the conspiracy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a>. In the Civil War it grew from several thousand members to several hundred thousand, while 60% of its pre-war members lost their lives. With Primo de Rivera in prison, Manuel Hedilla, a former mechanic, led the party until the merger of 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Twenty-Seven Points, drafted mainly by Ledesma, called for vertical syndicates uniting workers and employers by industry, land reform for the peasants, the nationalisation of credit and a ban on strikes and lockouts, and condemned what the party called usurious, financial and speculative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. They also called for the abolition of political parties and a nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. The militia, the Primera Línea, had a training manual for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, and the Sección Femenina, led by José Antonio's sister Pilar Primo de Rivera, kept the party alive underground in the spring of 1936. Its emblem was the yoke and arrows and its anthem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Falange Española de las JONS” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1937 Franco took control of the <b>Falange Española de las JONS</b>, whose founder José Antonio Primo de Rivera had been executed by the Republican government in November 1936, and merged it with the Carlist Comunión Tradicionalista into the Falange Española Tradicionalista y de las JONS, the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Cara al Sol&quot; (&quot;Facing the Sun&quot;) is the anthem of the <b>Falange Española de las JONS</b>, the Falangist party of the Spanish far right.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a></li></ul></section>
